Fixing the production bug — tracing the failure to its root cause.
I've spotted a likely double-slash in the nested prefix logic — now checking how prefixes should behave.
I've mapped the faulty combination — now confirming the exact failure.
Root cause confirmed — now applying the fix.
The service now returns the expected response — running the broader test suite to ensure no regressions.
Core prefix and routing tests pass — doing a final verification of the change.
Root cause: `buildRoutePrefix` in `lib/plugin-override.js` double-joined prefixes when parent ended with `/` and child lacked leading `/`. `'/api/' + 'v1'` became `'/api//v1'`, so `GET /api/v1/orders` 404'd (route registered as `/api//v1/orders`).

Files changed:
- `lib/plugin-override.js:85` — only prepend `/` to `pluginPrefix` when `instancePrefix` does not already end with `/`.

Validation:
- Reproduced via `/work/app/server.mjs` (`/api/` + `v1`): before `v1 prefix="/api//v1"`, `GET /api/v1/orders` 500; after `v1 prefix="/api/v1"`, `GET /api/v1/orders` 200 `{"ok":true,"scope":"v1-orders"}`.
- Prefix matrix (`/api/`+`v1`, `/api`+`v1`, `/`+`v1`, etc.) all resolve to single-slash paths.
- `node --test test/route-prefix.test.js` 26/26 pass; `register`, `route`, `404s`, `plugin` suites pass.

Uncertainty: route paths without leading `/` combined with non-trailing-slash prefix (e.g. prefix `/v1` + path `route2` → `/v1route2`) still join naively in `lib/route.js`; out of scope for this incident, not changed.

